{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 520,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s fair to say that black holes are\nthe scariest objects in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5700,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Happily for us, the nearest is probably many light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9370,
    "dDurationMs": 6144,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless of course Planck relics are a thing\n- in which case they might be literally everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20900,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes are scary because they’re so…\nfinal.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23700,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re surrounded by this surface we call\nthe event horizon, whose defining property"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28080,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that nothing that falls beneath it can\never emerge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31230,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if nothing can emerge from a black hole, then black holes must be A) black - they can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36320,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emit light, and B)"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37880,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eternal - they can only grow in size, never\nshrink."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41170,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, that’s the black hole as it appears\nin the mathematics of Einstein’s general"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45260,
    "dDurationMs": 1350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46610,
    "dDurationMs": 3789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But general relativity is NOT the whole picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50399,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back in 1974, a young genius named Stephen Hawking showed that if you bring quantum mechanics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56390,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the equation, quite literally, then the\nblack hole is neither black nor eternal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61790,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He showed that black holes must radiate, and so slowly leak away their mass in what we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66360,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now call Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67990,
    "dDurationMs": 8330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a way quantum mechanics saves us from the eternal black hole … except perhaps that it doesn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76320,
    "dDurationMs": 4329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you calculate the evaporation of the black hole all the way to the very last instant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80649,
    "dDurationMs": 6131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- quantum mechanics may well play a second trick and save that final speck of the black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86780,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole with the smallest conceivable size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91039,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These remnant black holes, or Planck relics, may be everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95829,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand Planck relics we need a quick review of Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100139,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is something we covered in gory detail previously, so for now just the parts that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105349,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need for today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106899,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking’s discovery of his black hole radiation was really ingenious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111469,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It came from thinking about how black holes interact with the quantum fields from which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115909,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all elementary particles arise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117979,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now to do this properly, you really need a\ntheory of quantum gravity, which we don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122450,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even have now and certainly didn’t in 1974."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126360,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Hawking used a sort of a hack - a brilliant workaround - to prove that if an event horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132090,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forms in a vacuum, then the vacuum states of the quantum fields have to be disrupted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137990,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To a distant observer it would look like the\nblack hole is radiating particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143870,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking even figured out what that radiation should look like: it should be thermal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148980,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The distribution of particle energies should\nfollow a blackbody spectrum, as though the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154270,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole has a real temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156420,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the common pop-sci description of Hawking radiation you often hear about particle-antiparticle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161830,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs appearing at the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164030,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One gets trapped, allowing the other to escape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165870,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is misleading; for one thing, the radiation doesn’t appear right above the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172520,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead the wavelength of the emitted particles are about the size of the whole event horizon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178720,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so they sort of emerge from the entire region surrounding the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182220,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the black hole left behind when a massive star dies, the event horizon is several kilometers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187640,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in radius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188660,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For such black holes the Hawking radiation\nis just photons - electromagnetic waves with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194110,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kilometers-long wavelengths, so really, really low energy radio waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200360,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a black hole would appear very cold,\nand would leak away its energy very slowly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205740,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as the black hole shrinks in mass and\nin size, its Hawking radiation also decreases"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210690,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in wavelength - but it increases in energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213990,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the black hole appears to heat\nup, and the evaporation rate increases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218569,
    "dDurationMs": 6171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This leads to a runaway process, and so the final stage of the evaporation should be positively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explosive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225740,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gives us this nice picture of a far,\nfar distant future in which the stars have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230780,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gone out and we only have black holes, which one by one vanish in bright pops of Hawking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236430,
    "dDurationMs": 1170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237600,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days most physicists agree that Hawking radiation is a thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242440,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Researchers have come up with different hacks for combining general relativity and quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246340,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics and they reached the same conclusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250010,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these are still hacks - approximations\nthat involve different assumptions, or only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256100,
    "dDurationMs": 3089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work in certain ideal circumstances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259189,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One big assumption is that the space near\nthe event horizon isn’t TOO strongly curved"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263499,
    "dDurationMs": 2771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compared to the smallest quantum scale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266270,
    "dDurationMs": 4149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When Hawking made that assumption, it enabled him to mathematically connect the high-gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270419,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region near the black hole with a very distant zero-gravity region where the Hawking radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275569,
    "dDurationMs": 2231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is observed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277800,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s reasonable for most sizes of black\nhole, but Hawking’s entire calculation falls"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284380,
    "dDurationMs": 4689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apart when the black hole has shrunk down to the tiniest quantum scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289069,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At that point you need a proper theory of\nquantum gravity to describe the process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293370,
    "dDurationMs": 4969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without that theory, we have no way to describe the final stage of black hole evaporation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298339,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- so it’s reasonable to ask - does that\nfinal pop really happen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304069,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think about it this way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305949,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A red hot poker glows because it has an enormous number of iron atoms, vibrating with every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311509,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313229,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that motion they produce thermal radiation that includes every possible wavelength of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317889,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318889,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you zoom in on a single iron atom - it\ncan’t emit every wavelength of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323499,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It jiggles in its little crystal lattice cage\nwith very specific vibrational modes, producing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329539,
    "dDurationMs": 2231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons of specific energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331770,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a large black hole is like our entire poker - there are many ways that the quantum fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337520,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can fluctuate around it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339680,
    "dDurationMs": 5609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The black hole loses energy one photon at\na time, but the process seems smooth and continuous."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345289,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as the black hole gets very small, the\nallowed vibrational modes start to get restricted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349759,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You no longer have a smooth, statistical spectrum to your thermal radiation - the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355589,
    "dDurationMs": 3541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will leak its remaining mass in sudden, discrete steps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359130,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at some point, there may be no allowed transitions that can take away the last of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364050,
    "dDurationMs": 1299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole’s mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365349,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In which case the Hawking radiation ceases, and the black hole becomes stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370169,
    "dDurationMs": 1691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when does this happen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371860,
    "dDurationMs": 4019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it happens at all, it’ll be when the\naverage energy of the Hawking radiation is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375879,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close to the entire rest-mass energy of the\nremaining black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380059,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, when you get to the point\nwhere a single photon would take away the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383539,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rest of the black hole’s mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385309,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that’s not an allowed transition, then\nthe remnant will become stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391009,
    "dDurationMs": 4481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This would be when the black hole’s mass\nis around 20 micrograms - what we call the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395490,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397080,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t sound like much but that’s\naround 2 billion Joules of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401330,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a black hole would have an event horizon of around 10^-35 meters - the Planck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408680,
    "dDurationMs": 5879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the size-scale where general relativity and quantum mechanics come into hopeless conflict,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414559,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and is sometimes thought of as the smallest\npossible chunk of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419300,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So might Planck-sized black hole relics, or\nPlanck relics, actually exist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424169,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, let me start by saying that we know\nfor sure that general relativity doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428539,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work on Planck scales, so even if Planck relics do exist they probably don’t look just like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433639,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mini versions of big black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436619,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But assuming that they a[re allowed in the\ntheory, are they also allowed in the real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441529,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442529,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only way to make black holes in the modern universe is in the deaths of massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448159,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449539,
    "dDurationMs": 7041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The smallest such black holes will take something like 10^66 years to Hawking-radiate their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456580,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire mass away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457990,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s much longer than the current age\nof the universe, none of these black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463680,
    "dDurationMs": 2319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will have become Planck relics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465999,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For Planck relics to exist now we need a way to make black holes that are much smaller than a star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471149,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A black hole with a mass of a billion tons\nor lower could have decayed to a relic by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476029,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now - and fortunately there’s a way to make those."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479569,
    "dDurationMs": 4091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s something we discussed before - primordial black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483660,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the extreme energies and densities near\nthe Big Bang, there are a few different ways"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487710,
    "dDurationMs": 4959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce enormous numbers of black holes, potentially of a wide range of masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492669,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some scenarios that allow extremely large numbers of very tiny black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499349,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we talked about recently, the most important consequence of having Plank relics from primordial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504249,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes is that these could potentially\nexplain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508279,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plank relics are really perfect candidates\n- they’d be completely invisible no matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513600,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how many of them were.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515700,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you’d need a lot to say the least because dark matter makes up 80% of the mass of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520621,
    "dDurationMs": 1129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521750,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may have been possible to create this insane abundance of black holes if they formed during"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526420,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the epoch of cosmic inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528600,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the hypothetical time just when the\nuniverse was expanding exponentially quickly,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533860,
    "dDurationMs": 2650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and can be thought of as the bang in the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536510,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back then, the density fluctuations may have been strong enough to generate crazy numbers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540680,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of tiny black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Besides explaining Dark Matter, another reason to want Planck relics to be a thing is that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547040,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they may solve a vexing difficulty that was\nintroduced with Hawking radiation - the black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552850,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole information paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554890,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We did a video on this also, but long story\nshort: if black holes radiate a perfect thermal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560590,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectrum then, by definition, that radiation\nhas maximum entropy and contains no information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565690,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about whatever fell into the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568250,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you completely evaporate a black hole and then all the quantum information that went into it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573780,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is deleted from the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576330,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That breaks one of the most sacred rules of quantum mechanics - the conservation of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580650,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581650,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if black holes never fully evaporate?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584670,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if all the information they eat is trapped forever in the tiny Planck relic?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589840,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, that’s some quality data compression."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592820,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also breaks another rule about how much information a given region of space can contain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597080,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the Bekenstein bound."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598890,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A way around this has been proposed - what if space inside black holes actually expands"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604670,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a region larger than the event horizon?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608060,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned this inflation thing earlier - what if at the singularity of a black hole a new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613150,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation is triggered?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615290,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then all that information would have plenty of room to exist, no matter the size of its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621220,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "package."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622220,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This conjures images of insane numbers of\nminuscule black holes swarming through the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625960,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe, and in each one a new inflation\n- a new universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630430,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, we’re getting a bit too far out there\n- which is saying something given that we’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635040,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talking about the most out-there objects in\nthe universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637090,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s get a bit more down to earth before\nwe wrap up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641190,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If Planck relics account for all of dark matter, are they around us right now?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645950,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I did a quick calculation and got that in\nan average city there might be … one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652680,
    "dDurationMs": 7190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just one Planck relic per 30km cube, and that’s enough to make up most of the mass in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659870,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660870,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because that’s how empty most of space is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664700,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now feel free to do the math yourself and tell me I’m wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667310,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or just ponder on the coolness that somewhere nearby there may be an actual black hole,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673070,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a tiny hole punctured in your neighborhood’s fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677400,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re covering comments from the last two episodes: the one about how it’s not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681940,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aliens yet, and then the one on how to mess with the limits of the uncertainty principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686140,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in detecting gravitational waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688060,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You guys had a lot to say about the value\nof pursuing the “it’s aliens” hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694210,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "verus our partially facetious mantra that it's never aliens. I want to clarify my position on this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698770,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want it to be aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700980,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I really do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702500,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s what makes me most wary about the aliens hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706950,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In almost every scientific investigation there’s going to be some hypothesis that you favor,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712340,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if you’re unaware of it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714270,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The effect of such a bias is that you can\ntend to interpret all evidence in the light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718850,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that particular hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720530,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s called confirmation bias, and its effect\nis insidious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725510,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the aliens hypothesis - this\none is particularly dangerous because literally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730120,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any unexplained phenomenon can be fit into some version of the extreme high-tech aliens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735140,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "story."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736140,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s as unuseful as invoking the supernatural to fill in the cracks of our understanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741280,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the extraterrestrial of the gap."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744370,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, as Simone Spinozi wittily queries: Aren't aliens also a natural phenomena?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750200,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, if they exist then they are, and that’s why we absolutely should keep an open mind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754270,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the hypothesis while at the same time being extremely cautious about the pitfalls"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759420,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of confirmation bias."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761500,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So saying “it’s never aliens” isn’t\nabout “not having the courage” to pursue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766420,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fringe idea - it’s about absolute scientific\nrigor so that when it really is aliens you’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know you did your due diligence and can have any faith whatever your conclusion actually is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777960,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that episode I mentioned the Sherlock Holmes quote; which taken literally is a sure path"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783110,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to hopeless confirmation bias."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785020,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goes: Once you eliminate the impossible,\nwhatever is left, however improbable, must"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789540,
    "dDurationMs": 1331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be the truth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790871,
    "dDurationMs": 4749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this assumes that you have the omniscience to imagine all possible hypotheses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795620,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "HeckNo offers a poetic modification: Once\nyou eliminate the impossible, you are left"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801180,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with all the things you didn't think about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803390,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you also commented on the release of supposed UFO footage by the navy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808030,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t worry - I’m not ignoring that - it\nwas released just after the episode was published."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812200,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m scratching my head over it and will\nsay more on it if I decide it’s warranted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816710,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, onto the episode where we looked at how the Heisenberg uncertainty principle can be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820470,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gamed to improve measurements - in particular in gravitational wave detectors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824370,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JG 46 wants to know how to build a divide\nto split photons to make them entangled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830570,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is done through a process called spontaneous parametric down-conversion, and is a behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835630,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of certain crystals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837410,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might know that regular laser light is\nproduced when an incoming photon causes an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841880,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron in a crystal to drop in energy to\nproduce an identical photon matched in phase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847050,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and direction of the first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849220,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In certain materials known as non-linear crystals, the incoming photon is absorbed and the energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853900,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is instantly emitted as two photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856870,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those photons are entangled with each other because various properties are correlated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860880,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- in particular phase, polarization, and momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864240,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are incredibly useful devices - as well as being used to create squeezed states of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868650,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light, they’re used in all sorts of other\nquantum optics applications like the various"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872700,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum eraser experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875060,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So LIGO themselves actually commented on this one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878190,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, not officially LIGO, but LIGO scientist\nMaggie Tse reached out to us to point out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883950,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an error."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885260,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We said that LIGO is planning to begin using squeezed light in a future run."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889150,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890150,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it was used for the first time in\nthe most recent observing run."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894100,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Tse is part of the squeezed light team,\nand led their paper on the subject where they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898840,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "report up to 50% higher detection rates, exactly as predicted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904090,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks for reaching out, Dr. Tse - and sorry\nwe were a little behind on the news."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908260,
    "dDurationMs": 5551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you criticized our title from last\nweek - we called it “Breaking the Uncertainty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913811,
    "dDurationMs": 6219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Principle”, but you rightly - if pedantically\n- pointed out that it should have been bending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920030,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or correctly using the uncertainty principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923530,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We stand corrected and make a note of which words are appropriate descriptors to use for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929150,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "squeezed light in regards to Heisenberg, to\nwit: bending - good, breaking, bad."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936150,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fake liner compliments my side-stepping skills - it’s as though I know exactly when text"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940030,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or pictures will appear before effortlessly\ngliding aside like a river-dancer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945070,
    "dDurationMs": 5769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks - by these are just standard tricks\nused in film … by us space aliens."
    } ]
  } ]
}
